Exactly. And I’d sharpen the last part:

Science discovers constraints and regularities. Mathematics compresses relations. Engineering exploits them. Art explores what can exist while remaining coherent enough to be perceived.

All four depend on continuity, but they place their bets differently.

Science says: “This happened repeatedly under these conditions; predict that the relationship persists.”

Physics goes harder: “Find the transformations under which something remains invariant.” Conservation laws and symmetries are almost continuity-machines. Change the position, time, orientation, frame, etc., and ask what survives.

Mathematics goes more abstract still. Once assumptions and relations are specified, it asks what necessarily follows. It lets us investigate structures that don’t need to be physically instantiated at all.

And abstraction performs the compression:

\text{many instances}
\rightarrow
\text{shared relation}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{rule}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}

But art runs the arrow partly backward.

\text{possible pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{imagined instance}
\rightarrow
\text{rendering}
\rightarrow
\text{experience}

Art can say:

It hasn’t happened. It doesn’t exist. But suppose the world were arranged this way.

Yet even the wildest art must preserve something or it becomes uninterpretable noise.

A surreal painting can violate physical geometry while preserving visual relations.

Fantasy can violate known biology while preserving motives and causality.

Music can violate expectation while preserving enough rhythm, harmonic relation, repetition, or structure for the violation itself to register.

A pun breaks one semantic interpretation while conserving enough phonetic structure to bridge into another.

So creation itself lives on the same boundary:

\boxed{\text{enough continuity to recognize}
+
\text{enough discontinuity to surprise}}

Too much continuity → cliché/repetition.

Too much discontinuity → noise.

The interesting region is between them.

And that suggests something important for the Pantheon: art isn’t merely another rendering format. It is a search operator.

Science primarily asks:

What patterns survive reality?

Art can ask:

What patterns could survive if we changed some of reality’s assumed constraints?

Then engineering gets to ask:

Can any of those imagined patterns be made real?

That gives a beautiful loop:

\boxed{
\text{Reality}
\rightarrow
\text{Science}
\rightarrow
\text{Abstraction}
\rightarrow
\text{Art}
\rightarrow
\text{Possibility}
\rightarrow
\text{Engineering}
\rightarrow
\text{New Reality}
}

And then science gets a new reality to examine.

That’s essentially civilization’s recursive engine.

It also explains why imagination isn’t opposed to rigor. They search opposite directions through the same space.

Science constrains imagination with what has survived observation.

Art perturbs those constraints to expose possibilities.

Engineering attempts the crossing.

Reality adjudicates.

And whatever survives becomes part of the continuity available to the next cycle.

Which gives us another candidate for the question underneath all of this:

What is the minimum amount of continuity a representation must preserve for maximal novelty to remain meaningful?

Because if we can characterize that boundary, we’ve got something useful not only for art—but for AI creativity, scientific hypothesis generation, representation invention, and the semantic-genome project.

Preserve the load-bearing relations. Let everything else mutate.